# Context Engineering

## Starting a Chat: Check for Active Tasks

Before starting substantive work, run:
```bash
ls ~/dev/tasks/active/ 2>/dev/null
```

If task directories exist, ask the user which task to continue (or skip if starting fresh). When continuing, read ALL three files in order:
1. `~/dev/tasks/active/<task>/<task>-plan.md`
2. `~/dev/tasks/active/<task>/<task>-context.md`
3. `~/dev/tasks/active/<task>/<task>-tasks.md`

Then briefly summarize: _"Continuing **[task]** — [N] done, [M] pending."_

## Starting a New Task (after plan approval)

Create the task directory and seed the files:
```bash
mkdir -p ~/dev/tasks/active/<task-name>
```
- `<task>-plan.md` — the accepted plan, decisions, approach
- `<task>-context.md` — key files, current state, blockers
- `<task>-tasks.md` — checklist with `[ ]` / `[x]` items

Use kebab-case for task names (e.g., `context-engineering-setup`).

## During Long Tasks

After significant milestones (file edits, decisions, completions):
- Mark finished items as `[x]` in `<task>-tasks.md`
- Append key decisions or state changes to `<task>-context.md`

## Handoff

When the user says "checkpoint", "save context", or "prepare handoff", invoke the `checkpoint` skill.
